# Line endings
#
# Without this file the working tree inherits whatever `core.autocrlf` happens
# to be set to on the machine. With `true`, git checks out CRLF, and prettier,
# which defaults to endOfLine "lf", reports every frontend file as
# misformatted -- measuring the checkout rule, not the files. Pinning the
# rules here makes the working tree identical on every machine regardless of
# local git configuration.
#
# Git stores LF for every tracked file. These rules only decide what lands
# on disk.

# Default: detect text, store LF, check out LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are read by cmd.exe, which is genuinely fussy about CRLF --
# labels and goto inside parenthesised blocks can misbehave in an LF-only
# file, and scripts/ here use both. Stored as LF like everything else, but
# written to disk as CRLF.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary. `text=auto` detects most of these on its own; naming them means a
# corrupt checkout is impossible rather than merely unlikely. The project
# handles photographs and spreadsheets, so these will appear eventually.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.xls  binary
*.zip  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# Generated and vendored files: keep them out of diffs and language stats.
uv.lock                    -diff linguist-generated
frontend/package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
